dApp files on Celo, you can use **Celo Composer**, a CLI tool that simplifies building, deploying, and iterating on decentralized applications[](https://github.com/celo-org/celo-composer "1"). Here's a quick guide to get you started:

1. **Install Celo Composer**: Make sure you have Node.js (v20 or higher) and Git (v2.38 or higher) installed[](https://docs.celo.org/build/quickstart "2"). Then, install Celo Composer using the following command:
   ```bash
   npx @celo/celo-composer@latest create
   ```[](https://docs.celo.org/build/quickstart "2")

2. **Set Up Your Project**: Follow the prompts to enter your project name, choose whether to use Hardhat (a smart contract development environment), and select a template if you want to use one[](https://docs.celo.org/build/quickstart "2").

3. **Edit Files**: Once your project is set up, you can start editing the files. For example, you might want to rename the `.env.template` file to `.env` and add the required environment variables[](https://docs.celo.org/build/quickstart "2").

4. **Run Your Project**: Navigate to the project directory and run the following commands to install dependencies and start your project:
   ```bash
   yarn install
   yarn dev
   ```[](https://docs.celo.org/build/quickstart "2")
   Alternatively, if you prefer npm, you can use:
   ```bash
   npm install
   npm run dev
   ```[](https://docs.celo.org/build/quickstart "2")

5. **Deploy Your dApp**: Once you've made your changes and tested them locally, you can deploy your dApp to the Celo blockchain[](https://github.com/celo-org/celo-composer "1").

